WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:04.830
Baby Driver’s five and a half minute opening
scene skillfully introduces us to the world

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:10.050
of the film, sets up some of the characters
and is one my favorite car chases in recent

00:00:10.050 --> 00:00:14.620
cinema. It does all this without any exposition
or dialogue.

00:00:14.620 --> 00:00:18.720
The scene has two parts. I’m going to talk
first about what Director Edgar Wright sets

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:22.710
up in the first half of the scene, and then
break down some of the techniques he uses

00:00:22.710 --> 00:00:25.160
for the car chase in the second half.

00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:27.189
Thank you to squarespace for sponsoring this
video.

00:00:27.189 --> 00:00:33.660
A good filmmaker will carefully use what the
audience sees and hears first to set up the

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:41.280
world of the film. Edgar Wright Does this
well in Baby Driver. Even before we see anything,

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:49.030
we hear a high-pitched tone that fades into
a musical note. The viewer won’t find out

00:00:49.030 --> 00:00:53.519
until much later in the film that Baby listens
to music because of his tinnitus, but the

00:00:53.519 --> 00:00:58.539
seed of that idea is planted firmly at the
beginning.

00:00:58.539 --> 00:01:03.859
In Baby Driver images are often subordinate
to sound, a reverse of the usual roles. So

00:01:03.859 --> 00:01:08.670
it makes sense that we hear sound before we
see images, establishing sounds critical role

00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:10.079
in the film.

00:01:10.079 --> 00:01:14.170
The opening three shots quickly and easily
give us context for the world and setup the

00:01:14.170 --> 00:01:15.560
main character.

00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:20.740
We see cars and a bank, a sign telling us
what city we’re in, and then Baby’s red

00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:26.329
subaru fills the screen, calling attention
to itself as the most important piece of this

00:01:26.329 --> 00:01:30.999
scene. A sharp attentive audience that has
seen any marketing material for the film,

00:01:30.999 --> 00:01:34.920
will probably be able to guess from this one
shot the scenario that’s being established

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:37.619
here, a bank robbery.

00:01:37.619 --> 00:01:41.890
The second thing we see is Baby’s ipod.
This is the first step in establishing the

00:01:41.890 --> 00:01:46.240
fact that the music in Baby Drivers soundtrack
is a part of the world. We’re hearing what

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:50.859
Baby’s hearing, the music isn’t just something
for the audience. By making this the second

00:01:50.859 --> 00:01:55.120
shot of the movie, Wright is also emphasizing
the important role that music will play in

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:56.759
the film.

00:01:56.759 --> 00:02:01.310
Finally we see Baby himself in a close-up.
Our main character.

00:02:01.310 --> 00:02:16.530
After Baby hits play on the ipod each of the
next four cuts happen on beat with the music.

00:02:16.530 --> 00:02:21.760
As the crew gets out of the car The slamming
of the car doors, opening and closing of the

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:34.129
trunk and their footsteps, as well as the
sounds of vehicles beeping and passing in

00:02:34.129 --> 00:02:39.069
the street, all synchronize with the music.
So in the first seconds of the film the audience

00:02:39.069 --> 00:02:43.760
is already introduced to the stylistic tic
that will propel much of the film.

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:47.730
At 48 seconds into the movie, the crew’s
masks go on and if you hadn’t managed to

00:02:47.730 --> 00:02:51.530
guess the situation yet it’s very obvious
now.

00:02:51.530 --> 00:02:56.819
This is where another important decision happens.
Even though the more interesting thing, the

00:02:56.819 --> 00:03:01.630
movement of the action is moving into the
bank, Wright uses a telephoto shot, and perspective

00:03:01.630 --> 00:03:07.970
to separate Baby from the crew, and we the
audience stay in the car with Baby.

00:03:07.970 --> 00:03:11.140
This not only gets us comfortable with the
fact that the rest of the story will play

00:03:11.140 --> 00:03:16.160
out from Baby’s perspective, it helps us
separate him from the seedy criminal world.

00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:20.860
Baby is told later in the film that he “isn’t
part of this work”, and it’s an idea that

00:03:20.860 --> 00:03:23.610
helps us sympathize with Baby’s situation.

00:03:23.610 --> 00:03:28.030
To further establish this, Wright uses a sequence
of Baby signing and drumming along with the

00:03:28.030 --> 00:03:34.420
song to juxtapose Baby’s attitude and demeanor
with what Baby is. Baby is a getaway driver,

00:03:34.420 --> 00:03:46.050
but he acts more like a bored intern passing
the time.

00:03:46.050 --> 00:03:55.540
Wright quickly reminds us however, that this
separation is just an illusion. The coincidence

00:03:55.540 --> 00:04:04.420
of a passing police car awakens Baby from
his reverie. And Baby’s view of what’s

00:04:04.420 --> 00:04:10.689
happening in the bank and the gunshot serve
as a harsh reminder for us and for Baby of

00:04:10.689 --> 00:04:12.310
what he’s involved with.

00:04:12.310 --> 00:04:17.800
The audience doesn’t know it yet but this
is the central conflict for his character.

00:04:17.800 --> 00:04:23.040
Baby’s concern about his relationship to
this world of crime. And it’s not accident

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:27.580
on the director’s part that it’s during
this section that the camera is the closest

00:04:27.580 --> 00:04:32.640
to Baby’s face that it’s been so far.
This close up emphasizes the importance of

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:35.100
this moment for Baby.

00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:41.070
Part II: The Getaway

00:04:41.070 --> 00:04:47.030
The second part of the scene consists of a
car chase. There are 164 shots spread over

00:04:47.030 --> 00:04:53.570
three minutes and 5 seconds. This gives us
an average shot length of 1.19 seconds. Other

00:04:53.570 --> 00:04:58.140
than the 23 second final tracking shot, the
longest shot during the chase is six seconds

00:04:58.140 --> 00:05:06.530
long. The shortest is 4 frames long. 1/6th
of a second long.

00:05:06.530 --> 00:05:11.720
This speed of cutting works here because the
scene functions almost more as a musical montage

00:05:11.720 --> 00:05:16.940
of a car chase, than as a real time depiction
of a car chase. There are many apparent jumps

00:05:16.940 --> 00:05:21.910
in time, some small some large, but this all
seems to work because of the strong role the

00:05:21.910 --> 00:05:29.280
music plays. The chase operates first as a
dance, choreographed to the music, and second

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:32.020
as a depiction of vehicles in geography.

00:05:32.020 --> 00:05:37.880
If you turn down the sound design and watch
the scene with just the music, you can see

00:05:37.880 --> 00:05:42.840
clearly how the scene is constructed in conjunction
with the music. It doesn't just complement

00:05:42.840 --> 00:05:57.060
the scene, it drives it forward.

00:05:57.060 --> 00:06:01.681
But it doesn’t lose a real sense of direction
either, there are many clues that allow the

00:06:01.681 --> 00:06:08.350
audience to follow the action, and the geography
can be picked up on in repeat viewings. But

00:06:08.350 --> 00:06:13.830
Edgar Wright prioritizes showing the feeling
of the scene over clarity of physical action.

00:06:13.830 --> 00:06:18.990
Like in this shot where we don’t see the
car swerve over into another lane, but we

00:06:18.990 --> 00:06:24.190
can almost feel it swerve because we can see
the movements and reactions of everyone in

00:06:24.190 --> 00:06:25.190
the vehicle.

00:06:25.190 --> 00:06:30.900
Of like this moments a few seconds later where
we never actually see the car make a u turn,

00:06:30.900 --> 00:06:34.940
but a shot of baby pulling the handbrake,
and the passengers bodies shifting in their

00:06:34.940 --> 00:06:39.180
seats as the car turns is enough to clue us
into what happened.

00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:43.940
And it’s striking, just how much time the
camera spends inside the car.

00:06:43.940 --> 00:06:51.061
59 shots of are of baby or the passengers.
While 78 shots are of the actual cars. While

00:06:51.061 --> 00:06:55.520
there are still more shots from outside the
vehicle, this 60/40 split still places much

00:06:55.520 --> 00:06:59.280
of the emphasis on what’s going on inside
the vehicle.

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:03.470
This chase to me lands closer to the effect
of being in a car chase like this, or driving

00:07:03.470 --> 00:07:08.571
a getaway car like this. It’s less like
the experience of merely watching one. Because

00:07:08.571 --> 00:07:13.780
of this it sacrifices some of the clarity
of geography, but for good reason. There are

00:07:13.780 --> 00:07:19.990
52 shots of Baby’s subaru by itself, and
30 shots of just Baby, the majority of the

00:07:19.990 --> 00:07:28.730
the scene is built around one thing. Baby
and his car. The police even take a huge backseat,

00:07:28.730 --> 00:07:32.570
only getting incorporated into about 25 shots.

00:07:32.570 --> 00:07:40.150
Often their lights appear as clear reminders
of the danger encroaching on baby. Or more

00:07:40.150 --> 00:07:46.430
subtly, in a reflection, to remind us they’re
right on his tail. But not much time is spent

00:07:46.430 --> 00:07:49.130
focus on that. Most of the time is spent on
Baby’s driving, and the passenger’s responses

00:07:49.130 --> 00:07:55.250
to it. Like this moment, which takes an early
step towards endearing us to the character

00:07:55.250 --> 00:08:00.270
played by Jon Hamm.

00:08:00.270 --> 00:08:06.020
Or these looks from Jon Bernthal’s character
that give us an early indication of his skepticism

00:08:06.020 --> 00:08:07.240
towards Baby.

00:08:07.240 --> 00:08:13.220
This to me is what sets this scene apart.
It’s singular focus, Baby and his Car, mirrors

00:08:13.220 --> 00:08:19.210
the singular focus of the film, Baby and his
Driving. The interior shots that focus on

00:08:19.210 --> 00:08:24.300
baby don’t just show him driving, they show
him making decisions that allow him to get

00:08:24.300 --> 00:08:29.802
away. We constantly see him assessing the
situation and adjusting. Often in chase scene

00:08:29.802 --> 00:08:35.539
like this, the lead car just seems to be following
a predetermined path. But in this scene it’s

00:08:35.539 --> 00:08:40.319
clear that they are able to get away because
of Baby’s clever driving.

00:08:40.319 --> 00:08:46.189
Baby’s car spins and skids through downtown
Atlanta, Wright constantly shifting the direction

00:08:46.189 --> 00:08:52.250
of action so that things don’t feel linear,
but winding and turning, more like a dance.

00:08:52.250 --> 00:08:57.339
Wright’s directing is showcased in this
scene. It’s not just a big flashy action

00:08:57.339 --> 00:09:03.060
opener to get you hooked. It does all that,
but while it does, subtly it feeds the audience

00:09:03.060 --> 00:09:20.279
information that will help them navigate the
story ahead.

